# Cache policy for static hosts that read _headers (Cloudflare Pages, Netlify).
#
# The default on Cloudflare Pages is `max-age=0, must-revalidate` for EVERY
# file, including content-hashed bundles — so each page load revalidates ~20
# chunks over the network. On a phone that is both slow and a reliability
# problem: every extra round trip is another chance for a chunk to fail.
#
# Vite fingerprints everything under /assets/ (name-<contenthash>.js), so those
# URLs are immutable by construction — a changed file gets a new URL. Cache
# them hard. Everything unhashed (the HTML shell, the service worker, the
# manifest) must revalidate every time, otherwise a browser can keep serving an
# index.html that points at chunk hashes a later deploy has already removed.
#
# IMPORTANT — precedence: Cloudflare Pages applies EVERY matching rule and, when
# two rules set the same header, the LAST matching rule wins. So the broad
# no-cache catch-all comes FIRST and the immutable /assets/* rule comes LAST,
# letting hashed bundles keep their long cache while everything else revalidates.

# The app shell for EVERY route — never cache. This must be a catch-all, not just
# `/` and `/index.html`: an SPA deep link (e.g. /crm/leads) is served the shell
# under THAT path, and _headers matches the request path — so a `/index.html`-
# only rule leaves deep-link HTML on the default policy. A stale shell there is
# exactly what strands a client on a build whose chunk hashes are already gone
# ("Failed to fetch dynamically imported module"). Covers /sw.js and
# /manifest.webmanifest too — a stale sw.js can never hand over to a new one.
/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# Immutable content-hashed bundles — most specific rule, and last, so it wins
# over the catch-all above. A changed file always gets a new URL, so caching
# these forever is safe.
#
# Caveat: when the SPA catch-all in _redirects answers a request for a deleted
# chunk with index.html, that HTML body picks up this immutable header too. It
# is harmless in practice — that URL belongs to a build that no longer exists,
# and the recovery reload moves the client onto freshly hashed URLs it will
# never request again.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
